No. The revised statement (the strong Markov property) does **not** imply that the event $\{B_{\tau_b+(t-\tau_b)}-B_{\tau_b}<0\}=\{B_t-B_{\tau_b}<0\}$ is independent of $\mathcal{F}_{\tau_b}$ for all $t\ge\tau_b$.

**Proof.**

Let $\tau_b$ be a stopping time (e.g. the first hitting time of level $b$). The strong Markov property asserts that the post-$\tau_b$ process

$$W_s := B_{\tau_b+s}-B_{\tau_b},\qquad s\ge 0,$$

is a standard Brownian motion **independent of $\mathcal{F}_{\tau_b}$**. Concretely, this means: for every **deterministic** $s\ge 0$ and every Borel set $A$,

$$\mathbb{P}\bigl(W_s\in A,\; F\bigr)=\mathbb{P}(W_s\in A)\,\mathbb{P}(F),\qquad \forall\,F\in\mathcal{F}_{\tau_b}.$$

Equivalently, the entire sample path $(W_s)_{s\ge0}$ is independent of $\mathcal{F}_{\tau_b}$, so any **deterministic** functional $\Phi\bigl((W_s)_{s\ge0}\bigr)$ is independent of $\mathcal{F}_{\tau_b}$.

Now examine the event in question. Since $\tau_b+(t-\tau_b)=t$,

$$\{B_{\tau_b+(t-\tau_b)}-B_{\tau_b}<0\}=\{B_t-B_{\tau_b}<0\}=\{W_{\,t-\tau_b}<0\}.$$

The crucial observation is that the **index** $t-\tau_b$ at which $W$ is evaluated is **random** and, moreover, **$\mathcal{F}_{\tau_b}$-measurable** (because $\tau_b$ is $\mathcal{F}_{\tau_b}$-measurable). Hence the event $\{W_{t-\tau_b}<0\}$ is **not** a deterministic functional of the path $(W_s)_{s\ge0}$ alone; it is a function of **both** the path $W$ **and** the $\mathcal{F}_{\tau_b}$-measurable random variable $\tau_b$.

The strong Markov property gives independence of $W$ from $\mathcal{F}_{\tau_b}$, i.e. independence of the *process* from the *sigma-field*. It does **not** give independence of $\{W_{t-\tau_b}<0\}$ from $\mathcal{F}_{\tau_b}$, because the random time $t-\tau_b$ couples the event back to $\mathcal{F}_{\tau_b}$. Evaluating an independent process at an $\mathcal{F}_{\tau_b}$-measurable random time destroys independence from $\mathcal{F}_{\tau_b}$ in general.

**Concrete counterexample.** Take $\tau_b=\inf\{s\ge0:B_s=b\}$ (first passage time to $b>0$), and choose $t$ deterministic with $t\ge\tau_b$ on the event $\{\tau_b\le t\}$. Consider $F=\{\tau_b\le t/2\}\in\mathcal{F}_{\tau_b}$. On $F$, the increment $B_t-B_{\tau_b}=W_{t-\tau_b}$ is taken over an interval of length $\ge t/2$; on $F^c\cap\{\tau_b\le t\}$, the length is $<t/2$. Since the distribution of $W_s$ depends on $s$ (variance $s$), the conditional distribution of $W_{t-\tau_b}$ given $F$ differs from that given $F^c$. Therefore

$$\mathbb{P}\bigl(W_{t-\tau_b}<0,\;F\bigr)\neq\mathbb{P}(W_{t-\tau_b}<0)\,\mathbb{P}(F)$$

in general, so $\{B_t-B_{\tau_b}<0\}$ is **not** independent of $\mathcal{F}_{\tau_b}$.

The revised statement only guarantees independence for **fixed** (deterministic) time shifts $s$, not for the **random** shift $s=t-\tau_b$ that is $\mathcal{F}_{\tau_b}$-measurable. Hence the implication does **not** hold.

### PROOF COMPLETE
